# The Maven wrapper is executed by /bin/sh on the CI runner. core.autocrlf=true on a
# Windows clone would otherwise be free to hand it back with CRLF endings, and a shell
# script with a trailing \r dies on its first line with an unreadable error. Pin it.
mvnw text eol=lf

# Its Windows counterpart is only ever run by cmd.exe.
mvnw.cmd text eol=crlf

# Spotless is configured with <lineEndings>GIT_ATTRIBUTES</lineEndings>, so the ending it
# enforces for a .java file is whatever git says that file should have. With no rule here it
# falls back to core.autocrlf, which comes from the Git for Windows installer rather than from
# the repository -- a contributor who sets it to input or false gets all 290 files reported as
# needing changes at validate, on a pristine checkout. Pin it so the gate is a property of the
# repository. The blobs are already LF, so this normalises the working tree, not the history.
*.java text eol=lf

# Prettier's `endOfLine` default is `lf`, and `npm run format:check` is a CI gate as of
# wave 1. With no rule here the working-tree ending comes from core.autocrlf -- which is
# set by the Git for Windows installer, not by this repository -- so a contributor who
# clones with autocrlf=true gets CRLF on disk and every file fails the format check on a
# pristine checkout. Same failure the *.java rule above exists to prevent, same fix: make
# the ending a property of the repository. The blobs are already LF, so this normalises
# the working tree rather than the history.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
# `scripts/contract-check.mjs` arrived in wave 2 and this line did not, so on a Windows
# clone that one file came back with CRLF and failed `format:check` on a pristine
# checkout — the exact failure the paragraph above exists to prevent, reintroduced by an
# extension nobody thought to list.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
